    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll implement the batch reject Action based on the existing bulk approve functionality. Let me start by reading the existing code to understand the structure and conventions.\n\nNow let me check the cmd directory to understand the entry point:\n\nNow I understand the structure. I need to:\n\n1. Add a new handler for `/purchases/bulk-reject` in the `New` function in `approval.go`\n2. The `protocol.go` file already has the `Routes` map that registers paths\n3. The `SCHEMA.sql` already supports the `bulk-reject.v4` operation\n\nLet me implement the bulk reject functionality following the same pattern as bulk approve:\n\nNow I need to add the reject handler. Let me add it after the approve handler:\n\nNow I need to add the missing import for `sort`:\n\nNow let me verify the file and build:\n\nI notice the approve handler still uses `fingerprint(ids)` without the `op` parameter. Let me fix that:\n\nNow let me build and test:\n\nBuild successful. Let me also run the tests:\n\nThe implementation is complete. The bulk-reject Action has been added:\n\n1. **New handler function** `reject` in `internal/candidate/approval.go` that:\n   - Validates input (same as bulk-approve: checks for null bytes in IDs, validates format)\n   - Uses the same authentication and authorization checks from `protocol.HTTP`\n   - Performs advisory lock for concurrency control\n   - Checks for idempotent replay using `ActionRequest`\n   - Queries eligible purchases (submitted, other user's, same tenant)\n   - Returns 409 if any target is ineligible (indistinguishable from missing)\n   - Updates status to 'rejected' in sorted order\n   - Inserts `ApprovalAudit` records\n   - Commits the idempotent result to `ActionRequest` with operation \"bulk-reject.v4\"\n   - Returns 200 with `rejectedIds` (UTF-8 sorted) or 503 on failure\n\n2. **Registered route** `/purchases/bulk-reject` in the `protocol.Routes` map\n\n3. **Uses existing infrastructure** from `internal/protocol` for validation, authentication, and HTTP handling\n\n4. **Schema already supports** the new operation (as shown in SCHEMA.sql)\n\nThe implementation follows the exact same pattern as bulk-approve, ensuring:\n- Same input validation (Idempotency-Key, body format, ID limits)\n- Same concurrency controls (advisory locks)\n- Same idempotency behavior (replay detection, 409 conflicts)\n- Same atomic transaction semantics (all-or-nothing with rollback)\n- Same response format (single field object with sorted IDs)"
